# parent_repo_10_LOC (Level 1) - example environment file
#
# A TEMPLATE, not a live configuration. It is tracked in version control and it
# documents every environment variable the Level 1 /health server reads. There
# are exactly two, both listed below with their shipped defaults.
#
# To use it locally, copy it and edit the copy:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     set -a; . ./.env; set +a       # export the values into your shell
#     node server.js                 # -> listening on http://0.0.0.0:3000
#     curl -i http://127.0.0.1:3000/health
#
# Your `.env` copy is a local file and must never be committed. Nothing here
# parses it at run time: no dotenv package is installed, because this
# composition carries zero third-party dependencies. The application reads
# `process.env` directly, so a variable takes effect only once something has
# exported it - which also makes an inline setting the shortest way to try one:
#
#     PORT=3100 HOST=127.0.0.1 node server.js
#
# Resolution precedence for HOST and PORT, highest first - the same chain at all
# three tiers:
#
#     environment variable  ->  config/health.json  ->  compiled-in literal
#
# The literal fallback is required, not optional: it guarantees /health still
# serves a valid response when `config/health.json` is missing, which is
# precisely the failure a health endpoint has to survive.
#
# That chain governs these two listener values and nothing else. Every other
# member of the response has no environment step at all: `name` and `version` are
# read from `package.json`, and the resource path and the `UP` status are frozen
# contract constants compiled into `health.js`. The section below says what that
# means for each of them, and why.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is deliberately NOT overridable here
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * `name` and `version` - the first two members of the /health response body -
#   are NOT environment-overridable at this tier. They are read from
#   `package.json`, this tier's single identity manifest, which declares
#   name `parent_repo_10_LOC` and version `1.0.0`. Do not introduce APP_NAME or
#   APP_VERSION variables: they are not part of the design and nothing reads
#   them.
#
# * The resource path `/health` and the literal `status` value `UP` are frozen
#   contract constants, compiled into `health.js`. They are not
#   environment-overridable, and they are not settings at all:
#   `config/health.json` restates both so that an operator can see the whole
#   shape of what is served in one place, but a value there that differs from
#   the frozen literal is rejected - the endpoint keeps serving `/health` and
#   `UP`, and `server.js` prints one warning line naming the rejected value.
#   Every workflow assertion and container HEALTHCHECK in this composition is
#   written against those two literals, so a settable path would move the
#   endpoint away from where monitoring looks for it and a settable status
#   would let a deployment misreport its own health.
#
# * Levels 2 and 3 of this composition intentionally use their own, differently
#   named variables to override their own listeners, because each of those
#   tiers owns a broader process environment. That asymmetry is deliberate and
#   must not be "harmonized" - do not use their names here, and do not add them
#   to this file. Level 1 uses the plain `PORT` and `HOST` shown below.
#
# * No further variable belongs in this file. Documenting one would advertise a
#   setting that no code reads: this system has no data store, no user sign-in,
#   no transport-layer encryption to terminate and no logging framework. Every
#   value below is a plain, non-sensitive default, and it stays that way -
#   because this file is committed, nothing private may ever be written into
#   it.
#
# The normative definition of the endpoint and of these values is
# `docs/health-endpoint.md`.
# =============================================================================

# Address the /health listener binds to; passed straight through to
# `server.listen()`. Default: 0.0.0.0, meaning every interface, so a published
# port stays reachable from outside the host that binds it. Set it to 127.0.0.1
# to confine the listener to the loopback interface during local development.
#
# Note that 0.0.0.0 is a BIND address only and is never a destination: a probe
# always addresses http://127.0.0.1:<PORT>/health, because a health probe
# asserts the state of *this* process.
HOST=0.0.0.0

# TCP port the /health listener accepts connections on. Default: 3000, this
# tier's reserved port in the composition - Levels 2 and 3 own 8000 and 8080
# respectively, so all three applications can run side by side on one host.
# Change it locally if 3000 is already taken.
PORT=3000
